# {{ benchmark_name }}-runner

Generation + eval container for the {{ benchmark_camel }} benchmark.

## Quick start

```bash
make install
# Edit runner/benchmark.py — implement load_tasks() and generate()
# Drop your dataset into data/
make docker-build
docker run --rm -it {{ benchmark_name }}-runner:latest \
    run --model gpt-5 --run-id smoke
```

## CLI

```
{{ benchmark_name }}-runner run --model M --run-id R [TASK_IDS...]
    [--skip-eval] [--problem PATH] [--dataset-file F] [--dataset-name NAME]
    [--results-dir D] [--service-url URL] [--parallelism N] [--timeout S]
    [--max-tokens N] [--temperature F] [--top-p F] [--top-k N]
    [--reasoning-effort S]
    [--custom-endpoint U] [--custom-api-key K]
    [--chat-completions] [--disable-streaming]

{{ benchmark_name }}-runner score --run-id R [--force]
```

## Service-loaded datasets

By default the runner reads tasks from the bundled dataset file. If the benchmark service exposes `/v1/datasets/{name}/tasks`, use `--dataset-name NAME` to fetch task definitions from the service instead:

```bash
{{ benchmark_name }}-runner run --model gpt-5 --run-id validation \
    --service-url https://<service-host> \
    --dataset-name validation
```

`--dataset-name` is mutually exclusive with `--dataset-file` and requires Descope auth through `VALS_AUTH_KEY`; legacy `BENCHMARK_API_KEY` bearer auth does not work on `/v1/*` endpoints.

If the service returns benchmark-specific task fields, define a `Task` subclass and set `TASK_MODEL` on the runner so fetched tasks are validated before `generate()` receives them.

## Environment

- `SERVICE_URL` — benchmark service URL (override with `--service-url`)
- `VALS_AUTH_KEY` (Descope) **or** `BENCHMARK_API_KEY` (bearer) — auth
- `{{ benchmark_upper }}_GENERATION_VERSION` — provenance stamp baked into generation.json
